    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d65">

                <pb facs="0144" n="978" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg978"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 600.119/620</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor for the Department of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Polk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Commercial
                        Adviser of the British Embassy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Crawford</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-02">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 2, 1918</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Sir Richard</hi>: I beg to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of March 18, 1918, enclosing a copy of a letter which you
                    addressed to Mr. Vance McCormick in regard to steps which are being taken by
                    neutral countries to exact artificial prices from belligerents for commodities
                    which they are in a favorable position to supply.</p>
                <p>In this connection I beg to inform you that, at a recent meeting of the War Trade
                    Board, it was resolved, after a discussion of the question of the control of
                    prices of exports to the northern neutrals, that until a detailed plan for
                    putting such control into effect should have been submitted, that Board was not
                    disposed to express any approval of the principle of the control of prices of
                    exports.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
